WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.316
File classification starts with what the
feature intends to accept.

2
00:00:04.316 --> 00:00:07.951
An extension allowlist and the multipart
media type can catch

3
00:00:07.951 --> 00:00:12.221
ordinary client mistakes, but both values
are supplied by the sender.

4
00:00:12.221 --> 00:00:16.037
A request labeled as a PNG still carries
arbitrary bytes.

5
00:00:16.037 --> 00:00:18.945
Define how the service handles mismatches
and unsupported

6
00:00:18.945 --> 00:00:21.943
types, and reject ambiguous results rather
than silently

7
00:00:21.943 --> 00:00:25.033
widening the accepted formats until some
parser succeeds.

8
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:26.486
Content signatures provide another

9
00:00:26.486 --> 00:00:28.485
signal by recognizing characteristic
bytes.

10
00:00:28.485 --> 00:00:32.211
They identify many common formats, but do
not describe every

11
00:00:32.211 --> 00:00:36.390
structure in the file or prove that later
processing is safe.

12
00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.752
Use recognition by the intended parser or
decoder, together

13
00:00:39.752 --> 00:00:43.296
with application specific checks, to
establish the supported representation.

14
00:00:43.296 --> 00:00:46.385
Successful decoding is useful evidence
about format compatibility.

15
00:00:46.385 --> 00:00:49.383
It is not proof that embedded content,
external

16
00:00:49.383 --> 00:00:53.018
references, metadata, or the parser
implementation itself are harmless.

17
00:00:53.018 --> 00:00:55.289
For a narrow image workflow,
canonicalization

18
00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:57.288
can reduce what is retained.

19
00:00:57.288 --> 00:01:01.014
Decode an explicitly supported format with
resource limits, enforce dimensions

20
00:01:01.014 --> 00:01:05.284
and pixel count, and encode a new
representation under server control.

21
00:01:05.284 --> 00:01:07.646
Retain only metadata the feature needs.

22
00:01:07.646 --> 00:01:11.008
If the original has no business purpose,
discard it

23
00:01:11.008 --> 00:01:14.461
according to cleanup policy and store the
derived image.

24
00:01:14.461 --> 00:01:18.186
The published classification and extension
should describe the actual output,

25
00:01:18.186 --> 00:01:22.366
and checks must apply to the exact bytes
selected for publication.

26
00:01:22.366 --> 00:01:25.819
This technique is specific to the chosen
media workflow.

27
00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:29.453
Re-encoding an image does not create a
universal sanitizer for

28
00:01:29.453 --> 00:01:33.178
PDFs, office documents, archives, or every
browser viewable format.

29
00:01:33.178 --> 00:01:35.359
Those formats need policies for their

30
00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:37.358
own structures and active capabilities.

31
00:01:37.358 --> 00:01:40.356
Keep decoders and inspection libraries
maintained, and isolate

32
00:01:40.356 --> 00:01:43.082
complex parsing where the risk warrants
it.

33
00:01:43.082 --> 00:01:46.081
Malware scanning can add another layer,
but a

34
00:01:46.081 --> 00:01:48.806
clean result does not prove complete
safety.

35
00:01:48.806 --> 00:01:51.805
The acceptance decision should combine the
business contract,

36
00:01:51.805 --> 00:01:54.531
content evidence, resource policy, and
intended downstream

37
00:01:54.531 --> 00:01:57.620
use rather than depending on one
convenient label.

